Firewall Basics

5.1 Concepts
A firewall filters traffic by rules (source, destination, protocol, port, state).
iptables is the Linux packet filter (modern systems also use nftables or ufw as front ends).
Tables: filter (default, allow or block), nat, mangle.
Chains (in the filter table): INPUT (to this machine), OUTPUT (from this machine), FORWARD (through this machine).
Targets: ACCEPT (allow), DROP (silently discard, scanner sees filtered), REJECT (discard and send an error back).
Rules are checked top to bottom, and the first match wins. If none match, the chain’s default policy applies.

5.2 Common Commands
sudo iptables -L -n -v --line-numbers          # list rules
sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT     # allow SSH (append)
sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j ACCEPT     # allow HTTP
sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 23 -j DROP       # block Telnet
sudo iptables -I INPUT 1 -s 192.168.56.50 -j DROP      # insert at top, block an IP
sudo iptables -D INPUT 3                               # delete rule number 3
sudo iptables -F                                       # flush all rules

5.3 A Safe Rule Set (allow specific ports, deny the rest)
sudo iptables -A INPUT -i lo -j ACCEPT
sudo iptables -A INPUT -m state --state ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT
sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT
sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j ACCEPT
sudo iptables -P INPUT DROP        # default deny

Add the ESTABLISHED rule and the SSH allow before setting the default to DROP, or you can lock yourself out.

5.4 Demonstrating a Blocked Port Scan
From Kali, scan the target (the firewalled machine): sudo nmap -sS 192.168.56.101. Ports show open.
On the target, apply DROP rules (or the default-deny set above).
Scan again from Kali. Blocked ports now show filtered and the scan takes longer.
Optional logging of scan attempts: sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 23 -j LOG --log-prefix "BLOCKED: " (view with dmesg or the system log).
Take before and after screenshots, since this is great demo material.